  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<para>For numbers 1–2, use the graph below:</para>
<image alt="This figure a shows a straight line graphed on the x y-coordinate plane. The x and y axes run from negative 10 to 10. The line goes through the points (negative 6, negative 8), (negative 4, negative 6), (negative 2, negative 4), (0, negative 2), (2, 0), (4, 2), (6, 4), (8, 6)." src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b43d81b1ae710bc8b78c0278758a16ed71d489d9"/><div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="0a202dbf-bfda-49f2-bdb0-f78c50c533fd" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--Enter Questions Below this line-->
  <!--Q1-->
  <exercise data-content-id="183419ea-c11a-4339-aadb-d9f6fddd56ed" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="(2,0)" data-solution-options="[&quot;(0,2)&quot;, &quot;(2,0)&quot;, &quot;(0,-2)&quot;, &quot;(-2,0)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>What are the coordinates of the \(x\)-intercept of the graph?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The graph crosses the \(x\)-axis at \((0,2)\).</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.11.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q2-->
  <exercise data-content-id="0ff195d9-131c-40b6-9d54-694f267a6025" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="(0,-2)" data-solution-options="[&quot;(0,2)&quot;, &quot;(2,0)&quot;, &quot;(0,-2)&quot;, &quot;(-2,0)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>What are the coordinates of the \(y\)-intercept of the graph?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The graph crosses the \(y\)-axis at \((0,-2)\).</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.11.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q3-->
  <exercise data-content-id="328040c7-4577-4c54-ba53-f3023e8a8ae7" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="(3,0)" data-solution-options="[&quot;(0,2)&quot;, &quot;(2,0)&quot;, &quot;(0,3)&quot;, &quot;(3,0)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>For numbers 3–4, use the graph below:</para>
      <image alt="This figure a shows a straight line graphed on the x y-coordinate plane. The x and y axes run from negative 10 to 10. The line goes through the points (negative 6, 6), (negative 3, 4), (0, 2), (3, 0), (6, negative 2), and (9, negative 4)." src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7825f9d07108bf3c79a1b240974ac6f172bdfca7"/> <para>What are the coordinates of the \(x\)-intercept of the graph?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The graph crosses the \(x\)-axis at (3,0).</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.11.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q4-->
  <exercise data-content-id="d9f6c4de-fe6a-4f3e-8663-849afb187a67" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="(0,2)" data-solution-options="[&quot;(0,2)&quot;, &quot;(2,0)&quot;, &quot;(0,-2)&quot;, &quot;(-2,0)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>What are the coordinates of the \(y\)-intercept of the graph?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The graph crosses the \(y\)-axis at \((0,2)\).</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.11.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q5-->
  <exercise data-content-id="3adaa38d-78ca-4063-bee4-3133a26edf43" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="(4,0)" data-solution-options="[&quot;(0,12)&quot;, &quot;(12,0)&quot;, &quot;(4,0)&quot;, &quot;(0,4)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>What are the coordinates of the \(x\)-intercept of the equation \(3x + y = 12\)?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! When \(y = 0, 3x + 0 = 12\) becomes \(x = 4\).</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.11.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q6-->
  <exercise data-content-id="99318492-7d7c-491b-b4ea-a460dfe1073b" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="(0,12)" data-solution-options="[&quot;(0,12)&quot;, &quot;(12,0)&quot;, &quot;(4,0)&quot;, &quot;(0,4)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>What are the coordinates of the \(y\)-intercept of the equation \(3x + y = 12\)?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! When \(x = 0, 3(0) + y = 12\) becomes \(y = 12\).</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.11.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q7-->
  <exercise data-content-id="b15f1dc1-aa05-4cbf-b354-2c2c6129903f" data-problem-comparator="math" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="\frac{3}{2}">
    <problem>
      <para>What is the slope of the line \(3x- 2y=6\)?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The slope is \(\frac{3}{2}\), calculated from the equation in standard form.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Remember the formula for slope in standard form. Use the Equation Editor to express your answer.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q8-->
  <exercise data-content-id="8179854f-8533-4972-b9f9-094d93ae10f2" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="2">
    <problem>
      <para>What is the \(y\)-intercept of \(y = 6x + 2\)? </para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! Compare your answer:</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.11.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q9-->
  <exercise data-content-id="fcec07ea-a807-4295-af28-e0b61a16035d" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="6">
    <problem>
      <para>What is the \(y\)-intercept of \(10x + 5y = 30\)?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! Compare your answer:</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.11.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q10-->
  <exercise data-content-id="5c1e1577-2cff-4350-8771-d6abb4e818c3" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="2" data-solution="a" data-solution-options="[&quot;a&quot;, &quot;b&quot;, &quot;c&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Which graph represents the equation \( 12=3x+4y \)? Explain how you know.</para>
      <para><image alt="" height="238" role="presentation" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a6168a6019660ea9b265ba1bac0348b9b39141e2" width="238"/>
        </para>
        <para><image alt="" class="img-fluid atto_image_button_text-bottom" height="238" role="presentation" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/3505fd868a59a904a12adbf1667c928455c2ebcd" width="238"/>
        </para>
        <para><image alt="" height="238" role="presentation" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/2a2eb8b183db689ad120aa6b36d0452cbbd0dfcf" width="238"/>
        </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! Graph a, because the intercepts are \((4, 0)\) and \((0,3)\).</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.11.2.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
